  "query_preview": "..(Tuesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 315 PM EST SUN NOV 22 2026\n\nThe 22/12Z model suite analysis beginning Tuesday morning shows\nshortwave ridging aloft extending from the Tennessee Valley to near\nLake Winnipeg, with an ~1037 mb surface high centered over the \nDriftless region. Anticyclonic flow around the high will be \nfeeding a chilly air mass (925 hPa temperatures near 0C) on low- \nlevel northerly flow. Meanwhile, further upstream, three 500 hPa \nshortwaves/vort maxes are noted: one over the Southern High \nPlains, a second crossing southern Saskatchewan, and a third over \nthe western Gulf of America.\n\nThe model suite is in good agreement showing the shortwave ridging\naloft propagating east of the JKL CWA during the day on Tuesday\nwhile the attendant surface high scoots to over the Mid-Atlantic.\nThe southern stream shortwave will then dig along the Gulf Coast \nTuesday night and Wednesday, sending moisture streaming northward \nacross much of the Southeast CONUS while a weak area of low \npressure initially takes shape in the vicinity of western Cuba \nbefore tracking toward the Florida Peninsula. Meanwhile, the \nnorthern-stream trough/closed low races southeast toward the Mid- \nMississippi Valley. From Wednesday night through Friday, these \nthree upper-level features phase to form a potentially sub-540 dam\nclosed low tracking up the Appalachians, mirrored at the surface \nby a strong Nor'easter slamming much of the East Coast with \npowerful winds and heavy rainfall.\n\nEastern Kentucky will be on the cold side of this developing low \npressure system, but the available cold air is limited in its \nintensity. LREF mean 850 hPa temperatures are not far from 0C in \nthe coldest air behind the low (though it is notable that this is \na downward trend from prior model suites). At this point, \nthicknesses and atmospheric sounding profiles suggest temperatures\nwill be just a little too warm for snow, though a few flakes \nmixing in cannot be ruled out. This will need to be monitored \nclosely, as further cooling in the guidance could yield more \nflakiness than currently anticipated. Overall, this system is\npresently favored to yield a miserably raw period of intermittent\nrainfall for the Thanksgiving holiday as well as the bracketing \nnighttime periods. Rainfall amount forecasts continue to increase,\nwith widespread 0.25 to 0.50 inch amounts now favored and \nsignificantly more (1 to 2 inches) possible in wetter scenarios. \nOnce the precipitation departs, heights rebound across eastern \nKentucky briefly next weekend as surface high pressure passes \nacross the Southeast US. The resulting southwesterly flow should \nallow for a temporary rebound in temperatures to above-normal \nlevels.\n\nIn sensible terms, look for a characteristically chilly November\nTuesday with highs in the mid 40s to near 50F, accompanied by a\nweak northerly breeze and mostly clear skies. Temperatures dip \nback to near freezing on Tuesday night before clouds increase on \nWednesday, along with late-day rain chances. Temperatures could \nrebound into the 50s on Wednesday ahead of the arrival of \nprecipitation. Conditions then turn rainy and raw from late \nWednesday through Thursday night with temperatures in the 30s to \naround 40F at night and probably only in the 40s on Thursday. A \nfew flakes cannot be ruled out, especially as precipitation ends \nThursday night. Dry conditions and a gradual warming trend follow \nfor next weekend with highs in the 40s on Friday, lower to mid 50s\non Saturday, and well into the 50s to perhaps nearing 60F in the \nwarmest spots on Sunday.",
